 "segments": [
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "Welcome to Kukhnya Season 2, Episode 12. Max looks back on the six months he has spent working at Claude Monet, recalling all the rookies who could not handle the heat and how people constantly come and go in the restaurant world.",
   "gap": 0.8
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Что я здесь работаю.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "chef",
   "t": "За те полгода.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Я заказывал цукини.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "chef",
   "t": "Это, по-твоему, цукини?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Ребят, я забыл заменить стоп-лист.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "chef",
   "t": "Это теперь твое место.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Ты придурок.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "chef",
   "t": "Кто так рыбу разделывает?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Ты что делаешь?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "chef",
   "t": "Кто рыбу разделывает?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Я тебя спрашиваю!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "chef",
   "t": "Но желающих устроиться тоже хватает.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Одни уходят, другие приходят.",
   "gap": 0.9
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "Right, let's take their exchange slowly and unpack the Russian.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Что я здесь работаю.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'That I have been working here.' Max anchors the line on 'what', while 'work' completes the thought.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "chef",
   "t": "За те полгода.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Over those six months.' In Russian, the idea of 'behind' flows naturally into 'half a year' without extra filler words.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Я заказывал цукини.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'I ordered zucchini.' Notice how Russian expresses 'I' and 'zucchini' with crisp inflection rather than helper words. Chef Viktor answers right away.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "chef",
   "t": "Это, по-твоему, цукини?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Do you call this zucchini?' In Russian, the idea of 'this' flows naturally into 'zucchini' without extra filler words.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Ребят, я забыл заменить стоп-лист.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Guys, I forgot to update the eighty-six list.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'kids' before landing on '86'd list'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "chef",
   "t": "Это теперь твое место.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'This is your station now.' Chef Viktor brings out the contrast between 'this' and 'now' in a few short words. Max answers right away.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Ты придурок.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'You are a moron.' The meaning hinges on 'you' working together with 'a jerk' in spoken Russian.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "chef",
   "t": "Кто так рыбу разделывает?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Who fillets a fish like that?' Chef Viktor anchors the line on 'who', while 'to butcher' completes the thought.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Ты что делаешь?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'What do you think you are doing?' Max anchors the line on 'you', while 'are you doing' completes the thought. Chef Viktor answers right away.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "chef",
   "t": "Кто рыбу разделывает?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Who fillets fish like that?' The grammar packs 'who' and 'to butcher' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Я тебя спрашиваю!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'I am asking you!' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'I' and finishing on 'am asking'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "chef",
   "t": "Но желающих устроиться тоже хватает.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Yet there are plenty of people eager to get hired too.' Notice how Russian expresses 'but' and 'grabs' with crisp inflection rather than helper words. Max answers right away.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Одни уходят, другие приходят.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Some leave, and others arrive.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'only' and finishing on 'come'. Now let's listen to the entire scene again without interruption.",
   "gap": 0.9
  },
